\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {2}{\ignorespaces Subject-level results under the subject-wise (leakage-free) protocol, pooled over the five disjoint test folds (100 subjects); mean $\pm $ standard deviation over 1000 bootstrap resamples of the subjects. Precision, recall and F1 are weighted by class support, so recall equals accuracy by construction. PD is the positive class.}}{4}{table.2}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:main}{{2}{4}{Subject-level results under the subject-wise (leakage-free) protocol, pooled over the five disjoint test folds (100 subjects); mean $\pm $ standard deviation over 1000 bootstrap resamples of the subjects. Precision, recall and F1 are weighted by class support, so recall equals accuracy by construction. PD is the positive class}{table.2}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2}{\ignorespaces (a) Subject-level accuracy of every model under the subject-wise protocol, for the left foot, the right foot and both feet. (b) The same models on both feet under the two protocols: replacing subject-wise splits by window-wise splits raises accuracy by twenty points or more without any change to the features, the models or the metrics.}}{4}{figure.2}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:results}{{2}{4}{(a) Subject-level accuracy of every model under the subject-wise protocol, for the left foot, the right foot and both feet. (b) The same models on both feet under the two protocols: replacing subject-wise splits by window-wise splits raises accuracy by twenty points or more without any change to the features, the models or the metrics}{figure.2}{}}
